<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adulis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adulis was an ancient port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. It was the main maritime gateway of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian highlands</a> to trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, the Roman world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Goods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> passed through it, and its commerce underpinned Aksum's wealth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adulis.jpg" alt="Adulis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byzantine_Basilica_(8527948107).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The port of <b>Adulis</b> on the Red Sea coast is described as a major hub for the export of ivory, tortoiseshell, rhinoceros horn and other African goods, suggesting that Aksum was already overtaking the declining Kingdom of Kush as a leading supplier of African luxury goods to Roman…</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its wealth came from the Red Sea trade between the Roman world and India, which passed through its port of <b>Adulis</b>, and from a productive farming country in the highlands.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aksum">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Aksum</a></li></ul></section>
